Let us define indicator random variables for each position: - For each $ k = 1, 2, \dots, n $, define an indicator variable $ I_k $ such that:
  $$
  I_k = 
  \begin{cases}
  1 & \text{if } \pi \text{ has a local maximum at position } k \\
  0 & \text{otherwise}
  \end{cases}
  $$

So: $$
\mathbb{P}(\pi(n-1) < \pi(n)) = \frac{1}{2}
$$

Therefore: $$
\mathbb{P}(\pi(k-1) < \pi(k) > \pi(k+1)) = \frac{2}{6} = \frac{1}{3}
$$

Simplify: $$
\mathbb{E}[X] = \frac{n + 1}{3}
$$

### ✅ Final Answer: $$
\boxed{\frac{n + 1}{3}}
$$
